WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.588
Real applications often need dynamic
queries.

2
00:00:02.668 --> 00:00:04.600
A search page may include optional
filters.

3
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.403
A dashboard may sort by several fields.

4
00:00:06.483 --> 00:00:09.094
A reporting feature may switch between
known report modes.

5
00:00:09.174 --> 00:00:11.262
Pagination may change limit and offset
values.

6
00:00:11.342 --> 00:00:12.595
Dynamic behavior is normal,

7
00:00:12.675 --> 00:00:16.330
but it needs a design that keeps caller
choices from becoming arbitrary SQL syntax.

8
00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.438
The first rule is that dynamic values
should still be parameterized.

9
00:00:19.518 --> 00:00:22.076
If a user selects a status, a date range,
or a numeric limit,

10
00:00:22.156 --> 00:00:25.080
the selected value should travel as a
parameter after validation.

11
00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:28.867
The fact that a query is assembled
conditionally does not change the value-handling

12
00:00:28.947 --> 00:00:29.208
rule.

13
00:00:29.288 --> 00:00:30.280
Values remain values.

14
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:31.821
Dynamic structure is different.

15
00:00:31.901 --> 00:00:34.773
Column names, sort directions, table
choices, selected fields,

16
00:00:34.853 --> 00:00:36.680
and report modes are part of the SQL
shape.

17
00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:40.363
Those decisions should come from
controlled allowlists selected by application

18
00:00:40.443 --> 00:00:40.756
logic.

19
00:00:40.836 --> 00:00:42.402
A caller can request a known option,

20
00:00:42.482 --> 00:00:45.093
and the application maps that option to a
trusted fragment.

21
00:00:45.173 --> 00:00:47.418
The caller should not provide the fragment
itself.

22
00:00:47.498 --> 00:00:49.899
Optional filters can be built from small
known pieces.

23
00:00:49.979 --> 00:00:53.321
For example, an application can maintain a
set of approved filter builders,

24
00:00:53.401 --> 00:00:56.272
each responsible for adding a known
condition and its arguments.

25
00:00:56.352 --> 00:00:58.388
This keeps the allowed query shapes
explicit.

26
00:00:58.468 --> 00:01:01.392
It also gives reviewers a concrete place
to inspect limits, joins,

27
00:01:01.472 --> 00:01:04.030
filter combinations, and
performance-sensitive paths.

28
00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.460
Search and pagination also need
reliability limits.

29
00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.881
Clear maximum page sizes, supported sort
fields, bounded search behavior,

30
00:01:09.961 --> 00:01:13.773
and predictable empty-result handling
protect both security and service stability.

31
00:01:13.853 --> 00:01:16.724
Safe dynamic query design is not about
eliminating flexibility.

32
00:01:16.804 --> 00:01:19.780
It is about making flexibility visible,
controlled, and testable.
